{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.72,"content_digest":"a1ef13c5922584b0e019335bf5a1ee38e67a7aedf8be8960dc550410757043d2","created_at":"2026-08-22T04:02:45+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[],"link_id":"foundation-link:6a1e2de3bcf36f3c061b8af5","meta_principle_id":"meta:computer-science:reduction-completeness","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:physics:01M0HGHKV9YC77T8FJ87S07RGG","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The NP-hardness of the entanglement-breaking problem is established by a polynomial-time reduction from a known NP-hard problem. The meta-principle that reductions transfer difficulty and guarantees is the general foundation for such hardness classification, and the specific channel-setting conditions are compatible with the abstract reduction framework.","relation_type":"supports","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T04:12:58+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0KTQZ6M68SKHPKARZR7EKFP"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T04:02:45+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"partial","confidence":0.62,"content_digest":"d5a8131b17a675cae0b86069fedc8328e1f0cce6e4156380e9e3ea5259a298d2","created_at":"2026-08-22T16:33:03+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"92b151d5e8cfb050a4e2188c6986b4299998ecc7d2d64455a4e17c327d5b782e","input_tokens":5980,"latency_ms":37394,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"39e4a20717a2dcf82f35edfba9994a6344f3542edfae4de4d5c7b91a6b5ac1a3","output_tokens":2377,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:7af5b0a632a857c0029733d6","meta_principle_id":"meta:foundations:uncertainty-propagation","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:neuroscience-cognition:01M0N51Z7K7DJBJA5FT4M1HN9Q","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The meta-principle states that uncertainty must propagate through the full reasoning chain. Hierarchical per-decision uncertainty estimation attributes uncertainty to each branch decision in a label hierarchy, thereby providing stage-level uncertainty information that a single flat per-voxel measure cannot. This is a compatible specialization of the general uncertainty-propagation idea, though the empirical capability claim itself is not derived from the meta-principle.","relation_type":"consistent_with","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T17:07:53+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0N72WTZDNS3VF5QZJB70PZ5"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"partial","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T16:33:03+00:00","variable_compatibility":"partial"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.6,"content_digest":"c821ec2798e6047d2e7356e9815d31b479757307a44284affdbe90090e57978e","created_at":"2026-08-23T06:51:22+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"cd1f0775fad95b10b15d0103b310c7d134928fd66ddfcc6074b1eb950059c455","input_tokens":5890,"latency_ms":33702,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"5a7dfd47bfaee1c6041cb9c65b57af5631937c351784b7747456809dd47525c4","output_tokens":2570,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:c4975ef69bd1bd2e54e2f3b1","meta_principle_id":"meta:foundations:triangulation-independent-bias","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:ai-ml:01M0PP5Q6MEDH921GVXC49VCWN","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The triangulation principle holds that repetition within one bias structure is weaker than convergence across differently biased methods. The principle's claim that repeated stochastic inference alone yields an incomplete uncertainty estimate is consistent with this: sampling variability from one model shares a single bias structure and therefore misses model bias and domain shift.","relation_type":"consistent_with","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T08:08:08+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0PTK9G6V6VXNVFWAWBHMSYT"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"partial","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-23T06:51:22+00:00","variable_compatibility":"partial"}
{"condition_compatibility":"partial","confidence":0.55,"content_digest":"0490d8c7af0e3e9504e1c8cb4e7666cd32313a847e90f96f8953b01e3652e705","created_at":"2026-08-23T04:22:56+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"e7cfe2eeecd4e3280a2d58721b927222f1f0745b7ec69823de83ca31f87c9369","input_tokens":6088,"latency_ms":32859,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"90306b13721975bac53b5e58efdf5e063fa6c3f4512df4d833088b2a096e27b0","output_tokens":2555,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:e4178ca755c55cc0a2ea3bf5","meta_principle_id":"meta:statistics-causality:central-limit","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:ai-ml:01M0PDNY666JQFZC406AQ4YFRK","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The central-limit theorem explains why aggregated fluctuations often approach a Gaussian form. Dropout at inference generates many stochastic forward passes whose combined perturbations can make normalized residuals approximate a standard Gaussian, matching the principle's observed residual-shape trend. The link is partial because the theorem does not guarantee Gaussianity for arbitrary dropout architectures or finite sample sizes.","relation_type":"consistent_with","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T08:08:08+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0PTK9G6V6VXNVFWAWBHMSYT"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"partial","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-23T04:22:56+00:00","variable_compatibility":"partial"}
